# The portfolio app's scene (docs/portfolio-plan.md): Python on the
# three desktop lanes; mobile remains unwired until packaging. The
# dashboard's dynamic tables first — each account copy stamps its own
# table, addressed by the shared authored id plus that copy's string
# key — then the TRANSACTIONS VIEW one push away, where 15,000 rows
# live in one For (docs/virtualization-plan.md §5).
#
# EVERY LEDGER STRING BELOW IS ARITHMETIC, not an observation:
# tools/gen-market.py writes guests/assets/market/transactions.csv
# byte-stably, the guest formats integer cents dollars-dot-cents, the
# tick posts POSTED on top, and tools/check-assets.sh re-derives the
# frozen halves from the CSV and that same posting rule on every run —
# so regenerating the artifact without moving this file fails at a
# gate, naming this file, instead of on three lanes.
#
# AND THE TWO SCREENS TIE OUT (ruled 2026-08-26): the ledger is
# generated to net to the dashboard's book, so `label@net` — buys minus
# sells over the rows showing, priced at the live book — repeats the
# dashboard's own money to the byte. Unfiltered it is label#0's
# "Portfolio: $10023.00"; filtered to Retirement it is label#5's
# "Account total: $2370.00", and it names the per-ticker quantities the
# positions tables freeze. check-assets derives both lines from the CSV.
#
# expect_window says the FIRST VISIBLE row and the DECLARED TOTAL —
# both data (ruled 2026-08-25). The realized band's WIDTH is the
# viewport in rows, a text metric no shared scene may sample; the
# compiled table-tier gate holds that arithmetic instead, the
# pane-ladder pattern. After scroll_to_row the first visible row IS
# the scrolled-to row, which is what makes it data on both the exact
# and the corrected paths.
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the
# scene-ready wait.
expect label#0 "Portfolio: $10026.50"
expect label#1 "Transactions: 15000"
expect label#2 "Brokerage"
expect label#3 "Account total: $7163.50"
expect label#4 "Retirement"
expect label#5 "Account total: $2363.00"
expect label#6 "Savings"
expect label#7 "Account total: $500.00"
# THE CHART (docs/canvas-plan.md §10), the artifact the canvas milestone
# exists to feed: the book valued at each of the last 90 days' prices,
# drawn by the CORE from an op stream this app computed, and blitted by
# whichever backend is under it.
#
# THE THREE VERBS ARE NOT REDUNDANT (§7). expect_drawing is the legible
# one — ops replayed and where the ink landed — so a failure reads as a
# diagnosis rather than as a hash mismatch. expect_drawing_hash proves
# this platform's libkaya produced the SAME PIXELS as the other two, down
# to antialiasing, and proves nothing about the screen. expect_ink is the
# only one that fails when the BLIT dropped.
#
# ONE STRING ON THREE LANES: the op stream is app arithmetic over
# guests/assets/market/prices.csv, which every lane stages and verifies
# by hash, and the coordinates are VIEWBOX units, so no platform's track
# can move one (§3.2, invariant 6).
#
# THE FIGURE IS INSET INSIDE ITS VIEWBOX — a left gutter for the money
# labels and a bottom one for the dates — so these four numbers can
# discriminate at all: a drawing that filled its box would read
# 0,0,100,100 whatever the transform did.
expect_drawing canvas@chart "220/1,4,98,91"
expect_drawing_hash canvas@chart "98ad246fcf87c572"
# THE BLIT, sampled at flat-region CENTRES and never at a boundary
# (expect_app_icon's measured discipline). 18,36 is plot ground high
# above the series and clear of both gridlines around it; 18,73 is deep
# inside the filled area beneath it.
#
# THESE ARE THE CORE'S OWN BYTES, and both modes are named because the
# DISPLAY raster uses the platform's appearance and kaya's palette has
# two of them. They are the SAME PAIR tools/scenes/canvas.steps freezes,
# and necessarily so: the same two paints over the same ground —
# `ground` alone, then `series_fill` composited over it — which
# crates/kaya/src/canvas.rs's the_scene_probe_points_are_opaque_and_pinned
# rasterizes and prints for both modes. Never retyped from a platform's
# read; this verb alone compares within ±1 per channel, because a macOS
# window's backing store carries the display's profile (§7.2).
expect_ink canvas@chart "18,36 18,73 = light FFFFFF/D2E3F7 dark 16181C/212A35"
# A DRAWING IS AN IMAGE to every platform's accessibility tree, and the
# numbers a screen reader is told are the app's own (§9). This line is
# also the chart's TIE-OUT said out loud: `now` is label#0's money to the
# byte, because the series' last point is the live book — the same
# discipline the ledger's `label@net` carries on the other screen.
expect_ax canvas@chart "image/Portfolio value, 90 days to 2026-08-24, $9730.10 to $10357.99, now $10026.50"
# THE DRAWN MARK beside the title, `fixed`'s forcing artifact
# (docs/canvas-plan.md §3.2.1, ruling 3). NO `expect_raster` HERE, and
# now for the structural reason: this chip is ungrown, so its track IS
# its viewbox, both of the verb's answers are true at once, and the
# core's own precedence (scene.rs's canvas_raster_shape) says "track" —
# measured, not argued, when the line was tried 2026-08-28. A policy is
# observable only where track and viewbox differ, which is why every
# canvas in tools/scenes/sizepolicy.steps GROWS; that scene carries
# `fixed`'s assertion, this one carries the drawing.
# Derived by crates/kaya/src/canvas.rs's
# the_portfolio_mark_expectations_are_derived, never typed from a
# platform's read: the op count and the ink bounds, then the byte-exact
# hash that says this lane's libkaya drew the same pixels as the other
# two.
expect_drawing canvas@mark "21/4,7,96,93"
expect_drawing_hash canvas@mark "29abce8483ccc343"
# THE CENTRE, and only the centre: this verb samples hundredths of the
# canvas's own BOX, and the day the mark grows, a `fixed` drawing sits
# centred in a track it does not fill — an edge probe would land in the
# margin then and say nothing about the mark. The colours are the pair
# the chart's filled region freezes, and necessarily: the same two
# paints, `series_fill` composited over `ground` (§7.2, ±1 per channel).
expect_ink canvas@mark "50,50 = light D2E3F7 dark 212A35"
# NO expect_window_size, ruled 2026-08-28 when the phone legs arrived:
# the declared 900x600 is a desktop request and a phone's window IS the
# device, so the outer geometry is the panes-band shape — a value the
# platforms legitimately answer differently, which no shared scene may
# sample. The scene's contract is the ADAPTIVE behavior (every
# assertion below is data-shaped and passed unchanged at 375x812 —
# the flex-presentation asserts moved to the cut tail 2026-08-30 when
# the grouped tier stopped laying those containers out);
# declared-size honoring is window.steps' mechanism proof, once.
expect_column_edges column@positions[brokerage] 4
expect_columns column@positions[brokerage] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_rows column@positions[brokerage] "AAPL,10,$180.00,$1800.00|NVDA,4,$950.50,$3802.00|VTI,6,$260.25,$1561.50"
expect_column_edges column@positions[retirement] 4
expect_columns column@positions[retirement] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_rows column@positions[retirement] "BND,20,$72.10,$1442.00|VXUS,15,$61.40,$921.00"
expect_column_edges column@positions[savings] 4
expect_columns column@positions[savings] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_rows column@positions[savings] "CASH,1,$500.00,$500.00"
# One copy's sort indicator and order move; both siblings stay put.
header_click column@positions[retirement] 3
expect_columns column@positions[retirement] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value ^3"
expect_column_edges column@positions[retirement] 4
expect_columns column@positions[brokerage] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_columns column@positions[savings] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_rows column@positions[retirement] "VXUS,15,$61.40,$921.00|BND,20,$72.10,$1442.00"
expect_rows column@positions[brokerage] "AAPL,10,$180.00,$1800.00|NVDA,4,$950.50,$3802.00|VTI,6,$260.25,$1561.50"
header_click column@positions[retirement] 3
expect_columns column@positions[retirement] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value v3"
expect_column_edges column@positions[retirement] 4
expect_rows column@positions[retirement] "BND,20,$72.10,$1442.00|VXUS,15,$61.40,$921.00"
# A second copy owns an independent sort state.
header_click column@positions[brokerage] 1
expect_columns column@positions[brokerage] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value ^1"
expect_column_edges column@positions[brokerage] 4
expect_columns column@positions[retirement] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value v3"
expect_rows column@positions[brokerage] "NVDA,4,$950.50,$3802.00|VTI,6,$260.25,$1561.50|AAPL,10,$180.00,$1800.00"
# The tick updates every nested collection, preserves each copy's sort,
# AND POSTS ONE DIVIDEND PER ACCOUNT — the two screens are one model,
# and label#1 counts the book from the same list the ledger is built
# from. A dividend carries no quantity, which is why the net line below
# is asserted on a book these three rows are part of. The view is
# CLOSED here, so this also pins that a tick with no view pushed writes
# to nothing that is gone.
click button#0
expect label#0 "Portfolio: $10023.00"
expect label#1 "Transactions: 15003"
expect label#3 "Account total: $7153.00"
expect label#5 "Account total: $2370.00"
expect_rows column@positions[brokerage] "NVDA,4,$940.50,$3762.00|VTI,6,$261.00,$1566.00|AAPL,10,$182.50,$1825.00"
expect_column_edges column@positions[brokerage] 4
expect_rows column@positions[retirement] "BND,20,$72.00,$1440.00|VXUS,15,$62.00,$930.00"
expect_column_edges column@positions[retirement] 4
expect_columns column@positions[savings] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value"
expect_column_edges column@positions[savings] 4
expect_rows column@positions[savings] "CASH,1,$500.00,$500.00"
# AND THE CHART FOLLOWED THE PRICES. The tick moves the series' last
# point and nothing else — the window, the axis band and every label are
# where they were — so the op count and the ink bounds are UNCHANGED
# while the hash moves. That pairing is what proves the hash sees what
# the bounds cannot (docs/canvas-plan.md §7.4), and it proves the
# redeclaration replaced the whole drawing rather than patching it
# (§2.1). `now` is again label#0's money to the byte.
expect_drawing canvas@chart "220/1,4,98,91"
expect_drawing_hash canvas@chart "6fc708cad68264c3"
expect_ax canvas@chart "image/Portfolio value, 90 days to 2026-08-24, $9730.10 to $10357.99, now $10023.00"
# THE TRANSACTIONS VIEW, one push from the dashboard. The four status
# labels are addressed by AUTHORED ID: their creation index sits behind
# every stamped cell the dashboard made, and a band decides how many of
# those there are.
expect_title "portfolio"
expect_entries 0
click button#1
expect_entries 1
expect_title "Transactions"
expect label@count "15003 of 15003 transactions"
expect label@first "first 2023-10-21 BND buy $841.44"
expect label@last "last 2026-08-25 CASH div $2.05"
# THE TIE-OUT, unfiltered: the whole book's transactions net to the whole
# book's holdings — the same six quantities the positions tables show,
# and the same money as label#0 above.
expect label@net "net AAPL 10, BND 20, CASH 1, NVDA 4, VTI 6, VXUS 15 = $10023.00"
expect_columns column@ledger "Date|Ticker|Side|Total"
expect_column_edges column@ledger 4
# The whole collection arrived: 15,003 rows of DATA behind a band that
# realizes a few dozen widgets. label@count counts what the guest holds,
# and the last three rows are the dividends the tick posted.
expect_window column@recent 0 8
expect_rows column@recent "2026-08-24,VTI,sell,$7547.25|2026-08-24,AAPL,sell,$7200.00|2026-08-24,VTI,buy,$7547.25|2026-08-24,VTI,div,$0.50|2026-08-24,VTI,sell,$10410.00|2026-08-25,AAPL,div,$2.40|2026-08-25,BND,div,$6.20|2026-08-25,CASH,div,$2.05"
# The band at rest sits at the top of a 15,003-row collection.
expect_window column@ledger 0 15003
# The middle, addressed by the ROW and not by a pixel: t07500 is the
# 7,501st line of the CSV, realized or not.
scroll_to_row column@ledger t07500
expect_window column@ledger 7500 15003
# The end. At a clamped scroll the first VISIBLE row is the viewport
# in rows below the last — a metric — so this scroll runs unasserted
# and the filter steps below carry the proof it happened.
scroll_to_row column@ledger t15002
# ONE ACCOUNT'S HISTORY: the filter narrows the DECLARED TOTAL under a
# live window: 10,305 rows leave the collection and the band re-fills
# from what is left. The account's last row is the dividend the tick
# posted for it.
choose select#0 2
expect label@count "4698 of 15003 transactions"
expect label@first "first 2023-10-21 BND buy $841.44"
expect label@last "last 2026-08-25 BND div $6.20"
# THE TIE-OUT, one account: Retirement's own transactions net to exactly
# what its card holds — BND 20 and VXUS 15 — and to label#5's money
# above, which is what makes "an account's holdings are the sum of its
# transactions" an assertion rather than a hope.
expect label@net "net BND 20, VXUS 15 = $2370.00"
# Scrolled to the FIRST row, so `first` is 0 whichever end of the band
# the tier reports — the one scroll in this file with no ambiguity.
scroll_to_row column@ledger t00000
expect_window column@ledger 0 4698
# A sort moves 4,698 rows THROUGH a fixed band: the order is data, the
# band is a position, and the header's indicator is the guest's.
header_click column@ledger 3
expect_columns column@ledger "Date|Ticker|Side|Total ^3"
expect label@first "first 2023-11-20 NVDA div $0.25"
expect label@last "last 2026-01-01 NVDA sell $44136.00"
# t01602 is the 2,001st row of the SORTED order — a row the band has
# never realized, reached by identity.
scroll_to_row column@ledger t01602
expect_window column@ledger 2000 4698
header_click column@ledger 3
expect_columns column@ledger "Date|Ticker|Side|Total v3"
expect label@first "first 2026-01-01 NVDA sell $44136.00"
expect label@last "last 2023-11-20 NVDA div $0.25"
# The net line is a SUM, so no order can move it: the same two positions
# after two sorts as before them.
expect label@net "net BND 20, VXUS 15 = $2370.00"
# t11545 heads the descending order, so the band that follows it is at
# the top of a collection whose order moved under it.
scroll_to_row column@ledger t11545
expect_window column@ledger 0 4698
# BACK TO THE DASHBOARD: the covered root is retained — its numbers and
# each copy's independent sort survive the round trip.
back
expect_entries 0
expect_title "portfolio"
expect label#0 "Portfolio: $10023.00"
expect label#1 "Transactions: 15003"
expect_columns column@positions[brokerage] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value ^1"
expect_rows column@positions[brokerage] "NVDA,4,$940.50,$3762.00|VTI,6,$261.00,$1566.00|AAPL,10,$182.50,$1825.00"
expect_columns column@positions[retirement] "Ticker|Qty|Price|Value v3"

# THE STACKED FOLD (docs/adaptive-layout-plan.md D7), at the scene's END
# so the phone cut costs nothing above it: stacked, the summary renders
# inside the ledger's viewport — one scroll, the summary scrolling away
# above row 0 — and side by side it does not. Every assert sits after a
# resize because the initial state is width-dependent (the adaptive
# scene's rule); the phone legs cut at resize_window and assert their
# always-stacked truth as a per-leg extra.
click button#1
expect_title "Transactions"
resize_window 900x600
expect_folded column@summary none
# THE FLEX-PRESENTATION ASSERTS LIVE IN THE CUT TAIL (moved 2026-08-30,
# with the grouped-screen rule): on iOS a table-bearing screen lowers as
# grouped SECTIONS — the detail and accounts columns dissolve into the
# stream and never lay out as containers, so "aligns" has nothing to
# read there. That is the tier's design, not a miss; these sample the
# FLEX presentation and run where it exists, exactly as the
# window-size note above scopes the outer geometry. The records read
# here were written at the scene's own 900 opening.
expect_aligned column@detail "stretch"
expect_aligned column@accounts "stretch"
# No expect_fills on column@detail: the breadth rule maximizes this
# column's height while the hugging dashboard legitimately leaves slack
# under its content (the empty-row ruling, docs/tables-plan.md).
# The hug half of that ruling: an ungrown summary table's viewport sits
# within a row-pitch of its last row — watched failing both ways
# (content-height frame removed, then inflated 100pt). BACK FIRST: the
# observation is generation-keyed and a covered dashboard cannot
# re-stamp it, so the read runs on the live screen, then the push is
# restored for the fold round trip below.
back
expect_entries 0
expect_fills column@positions[brokerage]
click button#1
expect_title "Transactions"
resize_window 640x600
expect_folded column@summary column@ledger
resize_window 900x600
expect_folded column@summary none
